Part B: Lab (90 min)

Step 1: Create a Docker Hub account at hub.docker.com. Remember your username (below, YOURUSER).

Step 2: Create the app
------------------------------------------------
bash
----
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/myapp && cd ~/k8s-journey/myapp

app.py:
--------

python
--------
from flask import Flask
import socket

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def hello():
    return f"Hello from v1! Served by container: {socket.gethostname()}\n"

app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)
----------------------------------------
requirements.txt:
-------------------
flask==3.0.3
-------------------
Step 3: Write the Dockerfile

dockerfile
-----------
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
COPY app.py .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
-----------------------
Note that requirements.txt is copied and installed before app.py. That is the cache rule in action.

Step 4: Build and run

bash
-----
docker build -t myapp:v1 .
docker run -d --name app1 -p 5000:5000 myapp:v1
curl localhost:5000

You should see “Hello from v1!” plus a container ID as the hostname.

Step 5: Examine the layers

bash
-----
docker history myapp:v1
docker images myapp
----------------------
Each line is a layer, with its size.

Step 6: Prove the cache works

Edit app.py (change “v1” to “v1.1”), then rebuild:

bash
-----
docker build -t myapp:v1.1 .

Watch the output: the base, requirements.txt, and pip install steps say CACHED. Only the COPY app.py layer rebuilds. Now move COPY app.py . above RUN pip install and rebuild twice, changing the code between. See how the slow pip install reruns every time. Put it back after.

Step 7: Tag and push

bash
-----
docker login
docker tag myapp:v1 YOURUSER/myapp:v1
docker push YOURUSER/myapp:v1

Open hub.docker.com and confirm your repository exists.

Step 8: Prove it works from the registry

bash
--------
docker rm -f app1
docker rmi myapp:v1 YOURUSER/myapp:v1
docker run -d --name app2 -p 5000:5000 YOURUSER/myapp:v1
curl localhost:5000
-----------------------------
Docker pulled your image from the registry, exactly what a Kubernetes node will do later.

Step 9: Add a .dockerignore (good habit)

__pycache__
*.pyc
